  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Very discreet and hard to spot from the road, Pramousquier beach unfolds a fine stretch of white sand and peacock-blue water, in a bay sheltered between Cap Nègre (which protects it from the Mistral wind) and the Pointe de l'Écuelle. The site also has history: in August 1944, Commandos d'Afrique landed here to destroy the German gun batteries on Cap Nègre, and Allied troops later used it as a secondary landing site.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on the town's other beaches, dogs on leash are allowed but banned from 8am to 7pm between 1 June and 30 September — a rule confirmed by the Golfe de Saint-Tropez tourist office for the Rayol-Canadel side. The beach is actually shared between two towns: the Fontalde stream, often dry, marks the administrative boundary between Rayol-Canadel-sur-Mer to the east and Le Lavandou to the west, which lists it among its "12 beaches". Best to check the on-site signage, as the two towns' posted rules may not perfectly align.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Voie Verte (former railway) from Canadel is the nicest way in on foot or by bike with your dog — about 2 km, shaded by pines, with no traffic.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-canadel">Canadel beach</a> is 2 km away via the Voie Verte. Towards Cap Nègre, the coastal path has been closed to the public since 2008 for security reasons (private property) — you can't continue the walk along the coast in that direction.</p>
    </div>

